<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crimean War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1853">1853</a>–<a href="/years/1856">1856</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Crimean War was fought from 1853 to 1856 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> and an alliance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. Much of the fighting centred on the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sevastopol">Sevastopol</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s defeat exposed its backwardness and prompted major reforms, including the emancipation of the serfs in 1861.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crimean-war.jpg" alt="Crimean War" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Simpson, artist (1823-1899); E[dmond]. Morin, lithographer (1824 - 1882), working for &quot;Day &amp; Son, lithrs. to the Queen&quot; … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Simpson_-_Attack_on_the_Malakoff.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The causes of the war lay in the Eastern question, the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s expansion in earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-wars">Russo-Turkish wars</a>, and the British and French wish to preserve the Ottoman Empire to keep the balance of power. The proximate cause was Tsar Nicholas I's demand that the Ottoman Empire's Orthodox subjects be placed under his protection. After the Ottoman refusal, Russian troops occupied the Danubian Principalities in July 1853, and the Ottomans declared war in October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> joined the Ottoman side in March 1854, after public outrage at the destruction of the Ottoman squadron at Sinop. In September 1854 the allies landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> to take the naval base of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sevastopol">Sevastopol</a>, won the Battle of the Alma, repulsed a Russian attack at Balaclava on 25 October, where the Charge of the Light Brigade took place, and defeated a second at Inkerman. The Siege of Sevastopol then lasted until 8 September 1855 and brought brutal conditions to both armies. Sardinia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> joined in 1855. Sevastopol fell after a French assault on the Malakoff redoubt. The British government of Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a> fell in January 1855 after Parliament voted to investigate the war's mismanagement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1856">Treaty of Paris</a>, signed on 30 March 1856, forbade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to base warships in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, made Wallachia and Moldavia largely independent, and gave Christians in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> a degree of official equality. Estimates of the dead vary widely: 95,615 French, 22,182 British and between 450,000 and 630,000 Russian, most from disease. The war was one of the first to use explosive naval shells, railways and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraphs</a>, and was reported by William Howard Russell and photographed by Roger Fenton. Its failures led in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to a demand for professional nursing, associated with Florence Nightingale. For Russia, defeat drained the treasury and became a catalyst for reform, including the emancipation of the serfs in 1861.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Crimean War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By staying neutral but hostile to Russia in the <b>Crimean War</b>, Austria lost the friendship of the power that had rescued it in 1849, while France and Piedmont-Sardinia combined against it in Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The western Circassians resisted for three more decades, encouraged by hopes of British and Ottoman help that never materialised in decisive form, even during the <b>Crimean War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Algerian soldiers fought for France in the <b>Crimean War</b>, the Franco-Prussian War and both world wars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A contingent sent to the <b>Crimean War</b> in 1855 restored its standing, and with French help it won at Magenta and Solferino in 1859.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Crimean War</b> the Treaty of Paris of 1856 returned southern Bessarabia and placed the principalities under a guarantee of the great powers, and the Paris Convention of 1858 allowed separate princes and assemblies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Crimean War</b> the Treaty of Paris put the Principalities under a tutelage shared by the Ottomans and the Great Powers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
